<GuidePage title='The Mental Model - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 3</div>
  <h1>The Mental Model</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    This is the pivot chapter. If you take one thing from this guide, take this: the model you
    learned from years of traditional React is not the model this stack is built on, and the
    ecosystem is moving away from it, not toward it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chapter 1 traced <a href='01-introduction.html#direction'>the direction of travel</a>: thirteen
    years of platform history converging on server-first. This chapter is the practical consequence:
    the model itself. What actually happens when an App Router page renders, and exactly where the
    old instincts betray you.
  </p>
  <p>
    And carry in the warning from
    <a href='01-introduction.html#dont-fight'>Chapter 1</a>: these are deeply opinionated systems,
    and <strong>the opinions are the price of the guarantees</strong>. What follows is not a style
    this guide happens to prefer. It is the model the platform's machinery assumes, and every
    deviation from it costs you some of the machinery.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='The shift, concretely'>
    <p>
      In an SPA, the browser receives a nearly empty shell and a JavaScript bundle; after the bundle
      mounts, each component pulls its own data into place, and the page assembles itself in front
      of the user. The question that model trains you to ask is
      <em>"how do I get data into my components?"</em> In the App Router, the page arrives already assembled:
      the server had the data and rendered with it before the browser saw a single byte. The only question
      left is
      <em>"which parts of my UI can't be finished on the server, and why?"</em>, and every such part
      is a cost to be justified.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='rendering-model'>3.1 What actually happens when a page renders</h2>
  <p>
    In the App Router, every component is a <strong>server component by default</strong>. When a
    request comes in, the server executes your component tree (running your
    <code>async</code> components, awaiting your data) and streams the result to the browser as HTML,
    immediately, including real content for everything that was knowable and reserved fallbacks for everything
    that wasn't. Client components (only the subtrees you explicitly mark with <code
      >"use client"</code
    >) are the only parts that ship JavaScript, hydrate, and become interactive.
  </p>

