    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The first page load, in the same visual language as the anatomy figure: blue is known and
      painted, amber-dashed is a reserved fallback, and the small ring is an interactive leaf. The
      server delivers the entire first frame as HTML (the blue arrow), then keeps streaming: each
      green arrow is one boundary's resolved HTML landing on its region, because every suspended
      region resolves and arrives independently. The two middle frames run at once: regions stream
      in (2a) while the JavaScript hydrates the already-visible leaves (2b), and the branches merge
      into the settled page. Nothing waits for anything it doesn't depend on: content doesn't wait
      for interactivity, and interactivity doesn't wait for the slowest fetch.
    </Fragment>
