  <p>
    Three consequences of this model are worth internalizing, because they invert traditional-React
    instincts:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Data access is a render-time concern, not a lifecycle concern.</strong> A server component
      doesn't "mount and then fetch"; it simply awaits what it needs while rendering. There is no timing
      to coordinate, no "is the data here yet?" state to manage. An entire category of client-side complexity
      (effects, request state machines, race conditions, stale closures) exists only on the other side
      of the client boundary. This is the deeper reason backend code has always felt simpler than frontend
      code: the server deals in one request → one response, while the client juggles user interactions,
      network variability, browsers, extensions, ad blockers, and time itself. SSR moves work from the
      hard side to the easy side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The bundle is the exception, not the app.</strong> Server components ship zero JavaScript.
      Your app's JS payload is exactly the sum of its
      <code>"use client"</code> subtrees, which is why boundary placement (<a
        href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a
      >) is an architectural decision, not a style preference.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Navigation re-renders on the server too.</strong> Client-side navigations fetch the new
      route's server output (the RSC payload), not JSON-for-you-to-assemble. Layouts persist without re-rendering;
      only the changing segments re-render. The router, not your state management, owns the page's identity.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id='app-directory'>The app directory is your app, not "routes"</h3>
  <p>
    It's tempting to see <code>app/</code> as the router's configuration area: thin
    <code>page.tsx</code> files that immediately hand off to the "real" components living in
    <code>components/</code>. That inversion is exactly backwards. The app directory
    <em>is the application</em>: its structure, its layouts, its containers, its core content.
    Everything related to the core parts of a page belongs there; the pieces that slot into that
    scaffold are what live outside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also the answer to a question worth asking yourself:
    <em>why does Next.js use file-based routing at all?</em> Because the filesystem makes the page's composition
    <strong>statically knowable</strong>. Layouts nest by directory. Loading and error boundaries
    are files, so the framework knows where they are before any code runs. Code splitting falls out
    per-segment for free. Prefetching a link means the router already knows exactly what the
    destination needs. Every one of those capabilities depends on the tree being declared in the
    filesystem rather than assembled at runtime in the browser. A five-line <code>page.tsx</code> that
    delegates everything to a giant client component doesn't just "move code around"; it hides the entire
    page from the framework.
  </p>

