  classDef cyan fill:#e0f5f9,stroke:#0891b2,color:#0e7490
</pre>

    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The same page, same data, two architectures. The SPA chain serializes five steps (with a
      spinner at each and a layout shift as each lands) before anything meaningful paints, and pays
      them again on every hard navigation. The server-first version parallelizes on the server and
      paints real content in one round trip.
    </Fragment>
  </Diagram>

  <p>And the failure modes compound, because each one blocks the escape route from the others:</p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Habit you keep</th>
        <th>Tool you lose</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Fetch after hydration (effects, client query hooks)</td>
        <td>
          Streaming and <code>loading.tsx</code>:
          <strong>there's nothing to stream</strong>; the server finishes instantly with an empty
          shell.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>"use client"</code> at the page/layout level</td>
        <td>
          Server components below it:
          <strong>the entire subtree is client forever</strong>; every child you add ships to the
          bundle.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Requests the server never sees</td>
        <td>
          The whole caching model: <code>"use cache"</code>, revalidation, request dedupe.
          <strong>You rebuild caching by hand</strong>, per hook.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Mutations via hand-rolled API routes</td>
        <td>
          Actions, <code>revalidatePath</code>/<code>revalidateTag</code>:
          <strong>read-your-writes consistency becomes your problem</strong>, solved with things
          like consistency tokens.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Client state for tabs, filters, visibility</td>
        <td>
          Shareable URLs, server-resolvable first render, and the router's back/forward behavior: <strong
            >all gone at once</strong
          >.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Prop drilling: the page fetches, layers pass it down</td>
        <td>
          Independent streaming and dedupe: components that can't ask their own questions
          <strong>can't suspend, stream, or cache on their own</strong>; every wire couples a layer
          to its parent's timing.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Dependent components (reads chained through <code>enabled:</code> or parent state)</td>
        <td>
          Parallelism: sibling reads that could have started together
          <strong>serialize into a waterfall</strong>, and the user waits for the sum.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Content that brings its own container and sizes itself on arrival</td>
        <td>
          Layout stability: with no space reserved up front,
          <strong>the page assembles itself in front of the user</strong>, shifting as each response
          lands.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Auth resolved in the browser (fetch the session, then decide what to render)</td>
        <td>
          The request already carried identity (cookies): the server could have rendered the right
          page immediately. Instead
          <strong>every page boots, asks who you are, and flashes the wrong state</strong> while it waits.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Read the left column against the Tracker codebase and the pattern is uncomfortable;
    <a href='18-applied-to-tracker.html'>§18</a> does exactly that, file by file. The point of this chapter
    isn't blame; every one of those habits was the correct professional instinct in 2019. The point is
    that they are <em>instincts</em>, and instincts don't update from warnings; they update from
    understanding the machine underneath. That's what Parts A through C are for.
  </p>

